          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does osteoarthritis guidance describe as core treatment before anything else?",
          "answer": "According to the osteoarthritis guidance (*Osteoarthritis: exercise-first management and the sequence of treatment options*, from rheumatology and orthopedic society guidance), the core treatment placed first is non-pharmacologic management, with structured exercise as the central part.\n\nThe guidance says exercise has the strongest and most consistent evidence of any intervention for knee and hip osteoarthritis. It describes a program that combines:\n- muscle strengthening around the affected joint\n- range-of-movement work\n- aerobic activity\n\nThe guidance says this is best delivered with supervision, at least at first, because sticking with it and progressing correctly affect the result. It also says exercise helps regardless of how severe the joint looks on imaging, age, or how much pain there is at the start. Some pain during exercise within tolerable limits is not a sign of damage, according to the guidance.\n\nThe passages I retrieved did not give details on the specific exercise doses or session schedules, and they did not cover how the later treatment steps are sequenced. For a personal plan, a clinician or physiotherapist would be the right person to ask.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
